A Introduction

We are pleased to provide the Alberta Department of Resource Development the"Department"with our initial report relating to affiliate relationships and codes of conduct

This initial report contains our preliminary observations and recommendations relating to the issue of the introduction of affiliate relationship rules in support of the onset of retail competition in the Alberta electricity industry Appended to this initial report is a draft strawman of an"Affiliate Relationships Code of Conduct"the"ARC"

In preparing this report,we have been guided by the following:


The issues raised for consideration in the March 31,1999 Request for Proposals issued by the Alberta Department of Energy as it then was and entitled Terms of Reference:Affiliate Relationships and Codes of Conduct the"TOR";


The objectives sought to be achieved,as articulated in
section 6 of the Electric Utilities Act,S.A. 1995,c E-5.5,as amended the"EUA",and the mischief sought to be avoided or remedied;



The need for a"made in Alberta"solution;and



The representations made by representatives of the Department and stakeholders,as described in sections B.1 and B.2 below,as well as throughout section C

1 Terms of Reference:The TOR identify seven key issues that should be considered in the articulation of affiliate relationship rules or codes of conduct for Alberta With the possible exception of the first issue,which highlights certain potential constraints arising from particularities of Alberta's legislative regime no divestiture,municipal profit corporation limitations and restrictions on the activities of rural electrification associations or REAs and which is qualitatively different from the remaining issues,the questions raised in the TOR informed and were specifically addressed as part of the analysis in arriving at provisions appropriate for inclusion in the draft ARC To avoid undue repetition,our comments and recommendations with respect to the issues raised in the TOR are,as appropriate,generally integrated within the scope of the discussion relating to various provisions of the ARC set forth below

As noted above,the first issue is of a somewhat different nature in that the constraints noted therein potentially affect the exercise as a whole rather than one or more of the discrete elements of the analysis That said,the legislated prohibition on divestiture,expressed more specifically in paragraph 3 1 of the EUA 1,and the constraints arising in respect of municipalities and REAs appear to be of relevance primarily to the question of the degree of separation between the wire services provider and its retail affiliate and are therefore considered in the context of the discussion set forth in section C.6 below

2 Objectives and Mischief:Rules and regulations are never developed in the abstract and their appropriateness in any given case falls to be gauged against both the objectives sought to be attained and the mischief to be avoided and/or remedied,as the case may be

Objectives:In the Alberta context,the objectives to be achieved by the restructuring of the electricity industry,of which the introduction of retail competition is one element,are set forth in section 6 of the EUA For present purposes,the relevant objectives are expressed as follows:

 

"c to establish rules so that an efficient market for electricity based on fair and open competition can develop in which neither the market nor the structure of the Alberta electric industry is distorted by unfair advantages of government-owned participants,




d1 to establish a framework that enables customers to exercise a preference for electricity services in a competitive market,and



e to establish a framework so that the Alberta electric industry can be effectively regulated where necessary and in a manner that minimizes the cost of regulation and provides incentives for efficiency"

That these are the objectives that underlie the exercise was highlighted during the initial stakeholder meetings conducted during the week of June 7 th,1999 2 To our knowledge,no stakeholder has taken objection to this approach

Two preliminary observations may be worth noting at the outset with respect to the objectives The first is that paragraph 6 c of the EUA speaks of distortion arising from unfair advantages The term"unfair"is of some importance in this respect,the implication being that"fair"advantages are not distorting and are not such as to require redress

The second observation is that there will almost inevitably be some degree of tension between potentially competing or conflicting objectives For example,codes of conduct,while assisting in the achievement of the objective set forth in paragraph 6 c of the EUA are apt,by their very nature,to increase rather than decrease the regulatory burden 3

Mischief:It is always useful to begin any regulatory design process by asking the following questions:

what mischief are we trying to guard against


who are we trying to protect and do they need regulatory protection


is the mischief likely to materialize


are the regulatory rules likely to be effective in eliminating the mischief or the likelihood of its occurring


What is the Mischief

One obvious mischief is the conflict of interest confronting a wire services provider that is involved,directly or indirectly,in both regulated and unregulated activities In its most direct form,the mischief is manifested by a lack of non-discriminatory access to its wires and services The mischief might also manifest itself in more subtle forms,such as where maintenance and repairs affecting customers of the wire services provider's affiliated retail business are being given priority There might be a cross-subsidy which flows from the wire services provider to its affiliated retail business the cost of which will be borne by all users of the wire service provider's system,including competitors of the retail affiliate or their customers There may be a temptation to offer one-stop shopping,tied connection and energy services or at least to create an impression that,somehow,the service offered by the wire services provider will be better,faster or more efficient if the energy is supplied by the retail affiliate

A second mischief relates to the release of confidential information Clearly,a retail affiliate of a wire services provider would have an unfair competitive market advantage if the wire services provider were to be allowed to pass to the affiliate commercially sensitive load flow or connection information that it had acquired solely as a result of its regulated functions Moreover,the issue here is not simply one of unfair advantage,as legitimate questions or expectations of privacy with respect to sensitive,customer-specific information arise as well

Since it is not the role of affiliate relationship rules to force customer choice indeed,the draft ARC prohibits transfers of customers without their consent except where required by law,we do not consider customer inertia to be a mischief for purposes of the matters under study in this report 4 That said,it is useful to bear in mind for purposes of this exercise that small retail customers in most utility industries are remarkably inert,particularly in the electric power industry where competitors find it difficult to offer sufficient value added to induce them to make a change Customers will predominantly only switch or buy on price and,where applicable,on the basis of the value added by combining and packaging together a number of services for example,offering cable,telephone,electricity and gas services as a package Margins for electricity retail competitors are remarkably slim by comparison to those available to retailers of other commodities

Thus,the primary mischief can be considered as comprising non-discriminatory access,cross-subsidization and the flow of information both as to access and privacy 5

Who Needs Protection

The role of regulation is primarily to protect the consumer interest with respect to price and quality of service Quality of service in the electric power industry is essentially a function of the control,operations,maintenance and repair of the transmission and distribution systems and the connection equipment There is already a regulatory regime in place in Alberta to protect the consumer for the purpose of ensuring quality of service by natural monopolies As far as prices are concerned,the interests of the consumer are best served by the forces of robust competition at wholesale and retail levels 6 Where prices depend on a competitive marketplace,the role is essentially one of oversight of competitiveness and the abuse of market power This oversight is conducted from the point of view of the consumer interest and monitoring,rather than interfering with,market forces

While few take issue with the statement that consumers require some measure of protection,a much more difficult question is whether competitors need protection If the objective is to create a level playing field,regulation should not seek to impose restrictions designed to protect inefficient competitors from the natural effects of market forces and competitive but not anti-competitive or abusive responses from market participants absent empirical evidence that competition might not exist without measures that will favour some competitors i.e.,new entrants while handicapping others i.e.,incumbent utilities Such restrictions will certainly inhibit market entry by incumbent utilities on the basis of incremental cost,which could otherwise create efficient competition at lower prices for the consumer That said,the competitive advantages enjoyed by incumbent utilities as a result of their historical franchised monopolies pose at least the potential for a threat to efficient competition The arguments can be summarized as follows:

 



From the perspective of the incumbent utilities,the arguments are apt to be to the effect that overly restrictive rules:

From the perspective of"new entrants"and some segment of the consumer population,the arguments apt to be to the effect that:

1 will severely inhibit competition in the retail market because incumbent utilities will not be permitted to enter the market at incremental cost utilizing existing resources-not allowing market entry at incremental cost is inefficient and disadvantages customers who would benefit from prices reflecting only incremental costs 7

1 the incumbent utilities are so firmly entrenched in the market that potential new entrants require a"leg up",so to speak,if there is to be any real or meaningful competition at the retail level,particularly in a small market such as Alberta

2 will create an unfair advantage for new entrants,that are free to use existing resources-this does not create the level playing field envisaged as necessary for the creation of competition-it is important to distinguish the concept of facilitation of competition from the protection of competitors from efficiency advantages of their rivals which benefit the consumer

2 the degree to which any economies of scale and scope are likely to avail to the benefit of consumers in terms of lower prices is questionablelower prices are more likely to result from robust competition than from economies of scope and scale 8

 

3 will eliminate any incentives for the achievement of economies of scale and scope in a variety of ways,again to the disadvantage of consumers

4 will interfere with or stifle natural market forces with the result that competition sufficient to protect the public interest will not develop

5 make it very unlikely that third parties will offer competitive billing and collection services 9

6 give rise to significant implementation and transaction costs

7 create an impractical,unnecessarily rigid and over-regulated regime

It is important to recognize that there is a critical policy decision that inescapably faces the Department in this regard;namely,how to strike the best possible balance between assuring a fair competitive opportunity to new entrants and limiting the ability of incumbents to take the benefits of efficiency advantages or economies of scale and scope Striking the appropriate balance goes to the very heart of the matters being addressed in this report and the Department's decision in this regard will dictate the eventual outcome of the provisions of the ARC,particularly those relating to separation

Likelihood of Mischief

It is not always easy to establish how likely it is that a particular mischief will,in fact,exist If it is very unlikely to exist,there is clearly no advantage in imposing restrictions and requirements unnecessarily since they tend to interfere with competitive freedom and market forces Given the current and past structure of the electric industry in Alberta,incumbent utilities have certain obvious advantages The presence of the incumbent utility in the same service territory as its retail affiliate raises market power concerns arising from ownership ties and the preexisting dominant market position of the incumbent utilities 10

Ten years of practical experience of competitive electricity markets in other jurisdictions indicates that many governments and regulators have concluded that there is a reasonable likelihood competition being distorted or non-discriminatory access not being achieved if appropriate rules are not put into place 11 The limited number of examples provided by stakeholders as to the present behaviour of certain of the incumbent utilities would tend to support this view 12

Effectiveness of Regulatory Rules

It should be noted that experience with conduct-based regulation has not been an altogether happy one The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission,in reviewing its standards of conduct for wholesale transactions,has noted that:

"we also believe that there is a great potential for standards of conduct violations that will never even be reported or detectedIt appears,based upon our experience thus far,that no matter how detailed the standards of conduct and how intensive our enforcement,competitors will continue to be suspicious that the wall between transmission operations and power sales is being breached in subtle and hard to detect ways The perception that many entities that operate the transmission system cannot be trusted is not a good foundation on which to build a competitive power market"13

That said,our inquiries in California have revealed that the affiliate transaction rules in place in that State are considered by the California Public Utilities Commission as effective,as evidenced by the small number of complaints that have been filed The introduction of standards of conduct in most other jurisdictions is too recent to allow for a meaningful evaluation of the effectiveness of the affiliate relationship rules adopted therein Moreover,it is not our task to revisit the issue of whether codes of conduct are or are not appropriate and effective measures versus divestiture,for example,as the results of this debate are already enshrined in the EUA 14

The issue does,however,highlight the importance of crafting rules that:

create incentives for achieving the desired behaviour;

 

are not easily circumvented;

 

are susceptible of being monitored for compliance;

 

can be meaningfully enforced;and

 

do not impose burdens on a regulator that does not have the resources to deal with them

 

3"Made in Alberta"Solution:We understand that,to be meaningful for Alberta,any recommendations must reflect the unique characteristics of the Alberta situation This includes a consideration not only of any legislated constraints,such as those identified above,but also of the size of the market,the existing industry structure and the relationships amongst market participants,as well as the identity of potential participants in the retail market and their respective potential market shares

A number of jurisdictions in North America have introduced codes or standards of conduct as a means of addressing the potential for the abuse of market power by incumbent utilities in the context of the introduction of retail competition There is a fairly high degree of consistency amongst such jurisdictions in terms of their approach to the matter,at least in terms of the broader principles Alberta is not the first jurisdiction to grapple with such issues and it can usefully have regard to the experience in other jurisdictions Nonetheless,while the lessons learned elsewhere can inform the process in Alberta,there is no substitute for creating rules that are responsive to the structure and concerns of a particular jurisdiction

B Methodology

This section of the report sets forth the methodology underlying our approach,including a synopsis of some of the concerns and positions expressed by stakeholders

1 Meetings with Department Officials:Our first order of business was to meet with various representatives of the Department with a view to sharing initial impressions,obtaining a clearer picture of the Department's objectives,soliciting information as to policy positions already taken and discussing the various initiatives being undertaken in respect not only of customer choice issues but of matters being addressed by separate working groups that may have some relevance to the matters at hand

We took away from these meetings the following elements,which as noted above have also informed the analysis and recommendations set forth in this report:

The objectives of the restructuring exercise include reducing regulation when possible and encouraging market forces where feasible

There is support for a retail market including competition amongst affiliates of the incumbent utilities and with new entrants

Work is ongoing elsewhere than under the auspices of this process with respect to identifying permitted bidders in the forthcoming auction of power purchase arrangements,retail settlements and default supply The draft ARC as is the case with similar codes in other jurisdictions does not address special affiliate relationship issues in these contexts

Subsequent discussions with representatives of the Department have revealed the following additional items of information:

The Department has made a policy decision to the effect that it will not prohibit retail affiliates from using their wire services providers corporate name or logo 15

While the Department may have expressed views or leanings in earlier documentation such as"Technical Papers"relating to other customer choice issues,these are generally indications of direction only and should not be understood as expressions of policy to which the Department has necessarily committed itself

2 Initial Stakeholder Consultation:Over the course of three days,we had occasion to meet with representatives of a number of Alberta's market participants We met individually with some and collectively with others 16

In advance of the commencement of the stakeholder consultation process,Enron had posted on the Department website a proposed code of conduct the"Enron Draft"Given that the Enron Draft is based largely on the provisions of the California affiliate transaction rules 17,which themselves appear to have formed the basis of the rules subsequently adopted in other jurisdictions in North America,we thought it a useful working tool to provide a checklist of issues and to focus and stimulate discussion 18 Accordingly,we encouraged stakeholders to express their views as to the substance of the Enron Draft as a means of ascertaining which issues might be the subject of consensus and which were apt to elicit a greater degree of controversy 19

The general tenor of the comments expressed to date are as follows:20

In general,the"new entrants"21 and customers share similar positions They advocate the need for a stronger code with specific provisions,clear separation of regulated and unregulated activities and strong and meaningful enforcement mechanisms They appear generally supportive of the Enron Draft in terms of minimum requirements and,in some cases,argue for even stronger or more restrictive rules 22 They do not support the Alberta Energy and Utilities Board the"EUB"or the Market Surveillance Administrator as the entity responsible for enforcement


Most of the incumbent utilities,on the other hand,would support a less stringent code based on broad principles rather than detailed rules,provided that the economies of scale and scope associated with vertical integration are retained A notable exception here is EPCOR,which is generally supportive albeit with some reservations of the Enron Draft Neither ATCO nor TransAlta supported the use of the Enron Draft as a starting point for the development of appropriate rules in Alberta From the overall tenor of the comments expressed by ENMAX,one might reasonably expect a similarly unenthusiastic response from that entity 23 Two incumbent utilities have provided copies of their own codes of conduct which provide an indication of the nature and scope of a code of conduct that they may be prepared to support 24 Those incumbent utilities that expressed a view on the matter favour strong enforcement mechanisms 25 They are also opposed to the creation of a new enforcement body for purposes of enforcement of the Code

We have not yet had an opportunity to meet or converse with representatives of the REAs or of their industry association This represents a potentially significant gap in our understanding of the issues of concern to Alberta industry members which it is hoped can be remedied in the next phase of stakeholder consultations

3 Timetable:Following consultation with the Department and stakeholders,it was agreed that this project would proceed on the basis of the following timetable:

circulation of a strawman of the ARC for consideration by stakeholders during the first half of July,1999,most likely towards the end of that period;


three weeks of stakeholder consultations respecting the ARC strawman,comprising an initial round of written comments,followed by face-to-face meetings with consultants,as necessary;and


final version of the draft ARC strawman to be completed no later than the end of August,1999,but sooner if possible 26

 

4 Scope of Report:As noted earlier,the scope of work for this project and,consequently,the scope of this report,is established by the TOR That said,it may be useful to enumerate some of the related issues that we understand fall outside the scope of our work:

the development of rules relating to the sharing of settlement-related information although the strawman ARC addresses the flow of information in generic terms,we understand that the more specific issues surrounding settlement-related information is being addressed elsewhere;


the development of rules relating to the specific information needs of independent power producers;27


the issue of the transfer of customers in relation to the stable rate option and default supply,which is being addressed elsewhere;28


the development of rules relating to energy usage data that wire services providers are expected to make available;29


rules relating generally to the qualifications and conduct of retailers vis-à-vis their customers,which is being addressed in separate documentation 30

All of the elements described above are in various stages of discussion and none have,as of the date of this report,been definitively resolved Resolution of these issues may well have a bearing on the matters addressed in the annexed draft ARC and some harmonization effort may well be required at a later date

C Strawman ARC

The remainder of this report is devoted primarily to a discussion of the provisions of the draft ARC annexed hereto For ease of convenience,they are presented in the order in which they appear in the draft We have not necessarily commented on all of the provisions of the ARC-some are quite straightforward and appear to require no editorial comment

1 General Approach:By way of general comment,our overall approach to the drafting of the ARC has been as follows:

      The question of the scope of application of the ARC raised the issue of the extent to which the ARC should extend to apply to all affiliates retail affiliates,other electricity affiliates,non-electricity affiliates or some combination of the foregoing of the wire services provider,which appears to be the approach underlying the Enron Draft Absent further information as to the need to extend the application of the ARC to all affiliates,we have preferred to begin by providing a relatively narrow focus in this regard From an economic perspective,the extension of the rules contained in affiliate relationship codes to affiliates operating in other markets such as telecommunications in which they have only a minority share or are proposing to enter for the first time has been referred to as a"truly ludicrous inversion of the infant industry rationale"31 From a legal perspective,it should be noted that the relevant section of the EUA contemplates the creation of codes of conduct between wire services providers and affiliated retailers and governing the flow of information between wire services providers and retailers 32 An"affiliated retailer"is defined as a retailer that is an affiliate of the owner of an electricity distribution system and a"retailer"is a person who sells or provides electricity services directly to consumers 33"Electricity services"is,in turn,defined by reference to an enumerated list of services pertaining in most cases more specifically to the electricity industry 34 There is therefore a prima facie question of authority to include in the ARC provisions governing the relationship between wire services providers and persons other than retailers or affiliated retailers in other words,affiliates engaged in non-retail electricity activities or non-electricity businesses,including those engaged in the provision of products or services that use or relate to the use of gas That said,paragraph 1 1 g.1 ix of the EUA does contemplate that the regulations may specify other services that are included in the definition of"electricity services",paragraph 31.995 1 p empowers the Minister to make regulations respecting any other matter the Minister considers necessary or desirable to carry out the intent of Part 3.2 of the EUA and paragraph 72 1 d empowers the Lieutenant Governor in Council to make regulations respecting any matter that the Minister considers is not provided for or is insufficiently provided for in the EUA Accordingly,the legal issue does not appear to be insurmountable Note,in this regard,that the definitions of"competitive retail affiliate","retailer"and"wire services provider"in the ARC are somewhat broader than the wording of comparable definitions in the EUA

Extension of at least some of the provisions of the ARC to non-retail affiliates conducting energy-related activities appears to be justified,particularly insofar as affiliates involved in generation are concerned Both ATCO and at least certain members of the new entrant/large customer group advocate such an approach Accordingly,although the annexed ARC applies only to the relationship between wire services providers and their competitive retail affiliates,parallel rules will be developed to address the relationship between wire services providers and their generation affiliates Should it be the case that all or substantially all of the rules applicable to the former are determined appropriate for the latter,the ARC will be amended to cover both Otherwise,a separate code will be prepared

The provisions of the ARC should be sufficiently clear so as to provide market participants with an adequate degree of certainty as to the rules that they are required to live by,while not attempting to be so prescriptive as to constitute an open invitation to be creative in finding inevitable loopholes


We generally do not favour the technique of articulating a broad principle,followed by examples of permitted and/or prohibited behaviour,which increases the likelihood of problems in interpretation 35 Moreover,the compliance plan mechanism which is proposed in section 8 of the draft ARC will afford market participants and ultimately the regulator an opportunity to ascertain whether the details of implementation are satisfactory

The rules set forth in the ARC may well be perceived as going beyond at least in terms of detail what may have been envisaged by the incumbent utilities 36 Given that international experience is not sufficiently mature to provide empirical evidence of which rules have proven non-essential or superfluous,we are of the view that some measure of prudence is called for in taking retail competition through its period of infancy We have considered,but ultimately decided against,an approach that would couple initial minimalist regulatory rules with a threat of greater intervention in the event of"bad behaviour"Such an approach,when considered in light of the customer inertia noted above,could well have the effect of allowing the incumbent utilities to position themselves now to achieve an unfair market advantage for use when the retail market opens Moreover,such an approach would almost inevitably require greater regulatory oversight The exemption mechanism proposed in section 13.1 of the draft ARC should assist in ensuring that rules which are,for one reason or another,inappropriate for a given wire services provider can be addressed on a case-by-case basis

2 Creation of a Level Playing Field:We believe that the rules set forth in the ARC can reasonably be expected to establish the level playing field sought to be created,in furtherance of fostering a climate in which competition may flourish while permitting the incumbent utilities to retain certain economies of scope and scale Although purely a matter of speculation,we would expect that the elements of real controversy will be the provision permitting the retail affiliates to use the wire services provider's corporate name and logo and the provisions relating to separation

3 Three Underlying Themes/Three Elements of Mischief:A review of the provisions of the draft ARC reveals that the provisions are intended to address the various elements of mischief described in section A.2 above;namely,non-discriminatory access to the wire services provider's distribution system and services;cross-subsidization;and access to information/consumer privacy While there is some overlap between sections in terms of the rationale for any given provision,section 3 generally relates to non-discriminatory access;section 4 to separation and therefore primarily with cross-subsidization but also information sharing;section 5 to transfer pricing and therefore cross-subsidization;section 6 to marketing and advertising addressing sharing of information;and section 7 to access to information and privacy The remainder of the provisions of the ARC address matters generally relating to implementation and enforcement

In addition,certain of the provisions of the draft ARC are intended to provide protection from attempts to favour competitive affiliates in a manner that would undermine competitive parity Even ardent advocates of restraint in the imposition of conduct rules recognize the legitimacy of rules such as those that prohibit the tying of competitive offerings to utility services as well as more subtle practices of a similar nature such as using contacts with utility customers to direct them to the competitive affiliate's offerings in preference to those of competitors 37

4 Section 1.1-Purpose:We consider it useful to include,as the first section of the ARC,a provision that states what objectives are intended to be achieved It is not possible at this time to anticipate every circumstance to which the affiliate relationship rules may apply A broadly worded"purposes"clause can go some ways towards ensuring that the provisions of the ARC are interpreted in a manner calculated to further the stated objectives

5 Section 3-Non-Discriminatory Access:Non-discriminatory access provisions are invariably part of affiliate relationship standards of conduct The provisions of section 3.1 prohibit a wire services provider from providing its retail affiliate,or representing to the public that it provides to its retail affiliate,any preferential treatment by comparison to its treatment of unaffiliated retailers

The provisions of sections 3.2 to 3.8 are designed primarily to ensure that the wire services provider does not provide its retail affiliate with any preference with respect to terms and conditions of service including price,availability of goods and services or access to customers Sections 3.4 and 3.5 pertaining to price tariff provisions and discounts,etc are desirable notwithstanding the other rules relating to transfer pricing,as the latter address cross-subsidization and may not be adequate to deal with the other concerns that underlie some of the provisions of section 3

With respect to section 3.7 tied selling,we are mindful of the comments made by ENMAX to the effect that such behaviour is addressed by federal competition laws Pursuant to section 77 of the Competition Act,tied selling is not a practice that is prohibited Rather,it is a practice that may be referred to the Competition Tribunal for review In other words,persons may engage in tied selling but are subject to having this practice reviewed in any given case Given the anti-competitive effect that tied selling may have and the difficulty of reversing the potential damage done as a result of tied selling,particularly in an infant industry such as the retail electricity sector,we believe that tied selling should be prohibited outright This is consistent with the approach taken in most of the codes of conduct applicable in other jurisdictions

6 Section 4Separation:We have included rules that require some measure of separation between a regulated entity and its affiliate rather than relying exclusively on cost-based mechanisms

Separation is an issue that has proven to be quite contentious in other jurisdictions Other than the provision relating to accounting separation,the separation provisions squarely raise the issue of the ability of incumbent utilities to retain the benefit of economies of scale and scope Unlike divestiture,which removes the incentive to distort non-discriminatory access,separation standards attempt to limit the ability of wire services providers to cross-subsidize their affiliates In theory,the greater the degree of separation,the less opportunity for anti-competitive behaviour and the lighter the need for regulatory oversight

Section 4.1-Corporate Separation:This section provides that the retail affiliate must be a corporate entity separate from the wire services provider 38 We note at the outset that both TransAlta and ATCO are generally opposed to corporate separation 39 while the new entrant/large customer group appears to be in favour by virtue of their support for the Enron Draft The SPPA,for example,has indicated that the greatest physical separation possible should be achieved

Corporate separation serves three primary purposes The first is to facilitate the application of the prohibition on cross-subsidies by ensuring a clear audit trail The second is that regulators have found it easier to achieve greater transparency and to monitor compliance in separate companies rather than within divisions of the same company The third is to permit independent governance of the retail affiliate in other words,absent a separate corporate identity,the affiliate would be governed by the same board of directors as the wire services provider Independent governance of the affiliate can be expected to compel the board of directors to more clearly focus on the business and interests of the affiliate,unencumbered by conflicts of interest which may result where the attention of the board of directors is focused on the interests of both regulated and unregulated activities Moreover,the board of directors will be monitoring compliance with the ARC from the single perspective of the affiliate and is,in theory,likely to pay greater attention to the rules and to compliance with same

We do not believe that the obligation to create separate corporate entities necessarily runs afoul of the provisions of paragraph 3 1 a of the EUA Requiring corporate separation arguably does not entail the transfer of property or the divestiture of assets where beneficial ownership remains the same albeit becomes indirect rather than direct In Ontario,for example,the relevant provision speaks both of disposing of assets and of undertaking a significant corporate reorganization Requiring corporate separation could be considered as violating the latter but not the former

Other legal issues arise with respect to corporate separation in the context of municipally-owned entities and the REAs More specifically,subsection 73 2 of the Municipal Government Act prohibits a municipality from controlling a corporation that operates for the purpose of making a profit unless it is authorized to do so pursuant to the regulations Control,in this context,is determined on the basis of a the holding of securities to which are attached sufficient voting rights,or b the fact of having appointed all or a majority of the directors Thus,unless an authorizing regulation has been made in respect of the municipally-owned wire services providers,corporate separation would appear to be legally problematic We understand that this prohibition is the subject of reconsideration but are not aware of whether a decision has been made in this regard Given the hierarchy of legislation,a regulation made under the EUA would not normally govern over a provision of the Municipal Government Act It appears that a relatively simple means of addressing this issue is to solicit the making of a regulation under subsection 73 3 of the Municipal Government Act that would permit municipalities to operate corporations for the purpose of retailing electricity,and it may be that certain municipalities have already taken steps in this regard While we would encourage efforts towards this objective,the Department is in a better position to gauge the wisdom of such steps or the likelihood of their success The REAs,for their part,also appear to lack the statutory authority under the Rural Utilities Act to create separate corporate retail affiliates

Notwithstanding the above,we have for the time being retained the provision requiring separate corporate entities With respect to the REAs,it appears that they could be exempt from this particular requirement 40 With respect to the municipally-owned wire services providers,to the extent that the issue cannot be resolved by legislative means as described above,they too could be exempt from the application of the corporate separation requirement We note,in this regard,that the behaviour of the municipally-owned utilities does not appear to be of particular concern to the new entrant/large user group of stakeholders That said,an approach that would effectively impose different rules for different classes of market participants would constitute a"checkerboard"approach that TransAlta has clearly indicated is not desirable

Section 4.3-Sharing of Facilities and Resources:With respect to the sharing of facilities and resources,we have not proposed to preclude a wire services provider and its retail affiliate from occupying the same building This would appear to be an unwarranted restraint on achieving economies of scale and scope Moreover,such a rule may create a competitive disadvantage for Alberta market participants in the sense that retailers having out-of-province affiliates including utilities may be permitted to share space in the same building as their respective affiliated companies The restrictions imposed in respect of the sharing of office space and equipment are,however,desirable to limit the possibility of cross-subsidization and the potential for conduct that would otherwise violate other provisions of the ARC relating to,for example,the sharing of information 41

The sharing of computer or information systems raises particular concerns in this regard We have therefore proposed to prohibit competitive retail affiliates from having access to the wire services providerscomputer and information systems We note that the Ontario Affiliate Relationships Code permits such sharing provided that appropriate data access protocols are in place Thus,we understand that it is technically possible to minimize the risk of unauthorized access by means of appropriate computer data management and data access protocols 42 It may also be technically possible to audit for compliance with access restrictions That said,compliance monitoring is apt to be difficult and of little value unless audits are performed on a frequent and regular basis Moreover,the sharing of passwords amongst employees is not an activity likely to be susceptible of detection We therefore do not recommend any sharing of computer or information systems

Section 4.5-Joint Purchases:Joint purchasing is not generally addressed as a specific element in the codes of conduct of other jurisdictions 43 Of those reviewed,only the California and New Jersey rules contain provisions relating to this issue,and both have addressed it by a prohibition on joint purchases that are associated with the"traditional merchant function"in other words,with the purchase of gas or electricity for resale,the purchase of electric transmission,systems operation and marketing

We recommend an approach that permits joint purchases by wire services providers and their retail affiliates provided that each party bears its proportionate share of the costs based on the benefit accruing to each from the joint purchase Again,there is a trade-off here between the consumer benefits that may arise as a result of less costly joint procurement resulting from the combined size of the wire services provider and its retail affiliate,on the one hand,and the use by the wire services provider of its market power to impede competition by giving its affiliate a clear cost advantage not available to competitors,on the other This may not be objectionable Moreover,retailers with out-of-province affiliates may have the ability to take advantage of procurement effected jointly with their respective affiliated companies

Section 4.6Separate Officers,Directors and Employees:We have proposed that the boards of directors of a wire services provider and its competitive retail affiliate be composed in part of independent directors 44 As noted above,independent governance of the affiliate can be expected to compel the board of directors to more clearly focus on the business and interests of the affiliate,unencumbered by conflicts of interest that may result where attention of the board of directors is focused on the interests of both regulated and unregulated activities Moreover,the board of directors will be monitoring compliance with the ARC from the single perspective of the affiliate and is,in theory,likely to pay greater attention to the rules and to compliance with same While we note that directors are required under corporate law to act in the best interests of the company,we do not believe such a provision to be superfluous in cases where conflicts of interest are almost inevitably going to arise In a number of jurisdictions,for example,membership on the board of directors of the independent system or market operator is limited,in whole or in part,to persons having no affiliation with any market participant 45 We note that ATCO has expressed its opposition to any mandated separation of boards of directors 46

The sharing and transfer of employees creates the potential for cross-subsidization and facilitates circumvention of the provisions of the ARC that prohibit the sharing of certain types of competitively advantageous information It is difficult to monitor against inadvertent information sharing and there is therefore merit in limiting the opportunities where such sharing can reasonably be expected to arise One cannot expect to erect and enforce a"Chinese wall"as between utility information and affiliate information contained in the mind of a single employee

We therefore propose rules,not dissimilar to those contained in the Enron Draft,primarily to limit the potential for information sharing These rules strike an appropriate balance between mitigation of the mischief,the retention of economies of scope and scale for employees other than those involved in operations,and the limitation of undue restrictions on employee mobility and advancement We note that IPCAA supported by the SPPA in this regard has recommended that the sharing of"key"employees be prohibited outright 47 This appears to be aimed at preventing what one might refer to as"intellectual cross-subsidization"Quite apart from the practical difficulty of identifying who are"key"and who are not"key"employees,we are of the view that such a provision constitutes an unnecessary interference with employee mobility and advancement IPCAA again supported by the SPPA in this regard has also suggested that the"CEO of each utility should be"encouraged"to write a letter to all employees,indicating that the code of conduct is a strong corporate policy,and if employees dont follow it they will suffer the consequences,most likely termination"While we do not consider it appropriate to include in the ARC a provision that addresses matters of internal corporate employment policy relating specifically to termination of employment,we have included employee education and discipline as matters to be addressed in each wire services providers compliance plan 48

An argument could be made to the effect that some premium should be paid by a retail affiliate for the transfer of an employee whose training has been at ratepayer expense Although the California rules include such a provision,49 the Enron Draft does not repeat it Such a provision does not appear to us to be warranted on the basis of economic principles 50

Section 5-Transfer Pricing:Cross-subsidization is the primary mischief sought to be avoided by the application of transfer pricing rules In other jurisdictions,this issue has been addressed in one of four ways:

Provisions specifying the price at which goods and services may be transferred between the incumbent utility and its affiliate,usually expressed by reference to a benchmark such as fair market value,fully allocated cost,fully loaded cost direct costs plus indirect charges and overhead or,where applicable,a regulated price This is the case with the rules applicable in California,Ontario,Pennsylvania,Massachusetts and New Jersey

Provisions requiring that transfers of goods and services between the incumbent utility and its affiliate be effected on the basis of a competitive tendering process The rules proposed for Texas embody such a mechanism

No express provisions relating to transfer pricing 51 This is the case in Maine and Oregon Illinois,for its part,prohibits all transfers of goods and services except to the extent such transfers are provided for and effected in accordance with agreements approved by the Illinois Commerce Commission

A provision that simply prohibits cross-subsidies,without elaboration as to price at which transfers are to be made This is the case with England and Wales

From the comments received to date from stakeholders,we are not convinced that transfer pricing rules are unnecessary or superfluous In addition,no stakeholder has expressly objected to the inclusion of such rules Indeed,ATCO is generally supportive of transfer pricing provisions 52 Accordingly,we do not advocate adoption of the third option

As between the first and second options,we recommend the first The competitive bidding process appears to present little by way of advantage over the first option,other than perhaps to provide a definitive mechanism whereby"fair market value"transfers can be assured It is,in our view,an overly and unnecessarily burdensome means of achieving the desired objective Moreover,the process may not be appropriate for transfers of goods and services from the affiliate to the retailer where arguably no mischief arises by virtue of a price that represents less than fair market value or for transfers of goods and services that are not intended for sale on the open market

We see considerable merit in the England and Wales approach,both for its simplicity and for the fact that it imposes on the parties an obligation to turn their minds to the adequacy of,and to justify,any particular pricing practice That said,we understand that past and existing experience in Alberta with cross-subsidization is such that this approach is not likely to be met with a great deal of enthusiasm among the non-utility stakeholders It may therefore,in the end,merely serve to create the need for additional regulatory oversight

Determining appropriate pricing rules themselves is a somewhat more difficult exercise The rules adopted in other jurisdictions,while to the same effect,are all slightly different California requires that goods and services meant for sale on the open market be transferred from a utility to an affiliate at fair market value and from an affiliate to a utility at no more than fair market value Transfers of goods and services not meant for sale on the open market are to be at fully loaded cost plus 5 of direct labour cost when from the utility to the affiliate and at the lower of fully loaded cost or fair market value when from the affiliate to the utility The New Jersey rules require that transfers from a utility to an affiliate of services meant for sale on the open market be at no less than fair market value and the benchmark used for transfers of other services is fully allocated cost rather than fully loaded cost Transfers of assets from a utility to its affiliate are required to be recorded at the greater of book cost or fair market value while those from an affiliate to the utility must be recorded at the lesser of book value or fair market value The rules applicable to PP&L in Pennsylvania require that transfers to affiliated suppliers be at the higher of cost or market price while transfers in the opposite direction cannot be at higher than the market price In Ontario transfers from the utility to the affiliate are required to be at fair market value while those in the opposite direction are to be at"no less than"fair market value In Massachusetts,transfers of assets and services from an affiliate to a utility are required to be at no greater than market value Transfers from a utility to its affiliate of assets the cost of which has been reflected in the utility's rates for regulated service must be at the higher of net book value or market value while transfers of other assets and services are to be equal to or greater than the utility's fully allocated cost to provide the asset or service

The pricing transfer rules that we recommend are similar to those contained in the Enron Draft which did not include the 5 gross-up in fully loaded cost We do not believe that fair market value is apt to be challenged as an appropriate benchmark Moreover,it has the benefit of being consistent with the provisions of the federal Income Tax Act pertaining to transfers of assets between related parties 53 With respect to the inclusion of fully loaded costs as opposed to fair market value or fully allocated costs as a benchmark for certain transfers,we have again been sensitive to concerns expressed with respect to existing cross-subsidization practices That said,we also note the following comments regarding the fully allocated cost alternative:

 

"From this standpoint,a requirement instead that the transfers be at fully allocated,average costs would be unduly generous to patrons of the regulated operations and inefficiently protective of competitors of the unregulated At the same time,it has the attractiveness,from the regulatory perspective,of permitting purchasers of the utility services to share proportionately in the benefits of those economies,which service to them has made possible It has the additional attractiveness of administrative convenience and unequivocal effectiveness in protecting both competitors and utility customers from cross-subsidizationWhile full cost allocations,resulting in pricing inputs supplied by the utility company to its unregulated operations markedly above the bare incremental costs that pure economic efficiency and efficient competitive would dictate,may serve as a rough-and-ready way of permitting purchasers of the regulated services to shareequitablyin the economies of scope,they will,predictably,not satisfy the complaints by competitors that the resulting competition isunfair"54

We believe that it would be helpful to seek the views of economists and/or accountants from within the Department and,if appropriate,the EUB,with respect to the appropriateness of fully loaded cost as an additional benchmark

8 Section 6Marketing and Advertising:These provisions are designed to limit the opportunities for subtle forms of tying,preferential treatment and cross-subsidization,as well as the likelihood of customer confusion as to the association between the retail affiliate's products and services with those of the wire services provider,that may be created by joint marketing and advertising efforts For example,portions of section 6.5 prohibit conduct by a wire services provider that would,if not circumscribed,allow the wire services provider to use information gained in the conduct of its monopoly utility functions to provide the affiliate with an unfair advantage By way of example,but for these and other rules see section 7.5,a wire services provider that is approached by an unaffiliated retailer for wires-related information pertaining to a particular customer assuming that the consent of the customer to disclosure has been given could advise its affiliated retailer of the request The affiliated retailer would then be in a position to use this information to approach the customer in question and possibly pre-empt the non-affiliated retailers efforts

The provision most apt to be the subject of controversy amongst stakeholders relates to the use by a retail affiliate of a wire services provider's corporate,name and logo We understand that this issue has already been the subject of a policy decision by the Department to the effect that such use will not be prohibited For the reasons noted below,we are supportive of such a policy

The Enron Draft proposes,and IPCAA and the SPPA support,a prohibition on the use by the affiliate of the wire services providers name and logo ENMAX,EPCOR,TransAlta and the City of Red Deer have voiced their opposition to such a prohibition and one could reasonably expect other wire services providers to do the same Codes of conduct used in other jurisdictions,including the California rules on which the Enron Draft draws heavily,rarely take such an extreme position Rather,they permit such use with the proviso that appropriate disclaimers are used to prevent any customer confusion that may arise as to the association between the wire services provider and the retail affiliate This,in our view,adequately protects against the real mischief at hand So far as economic principles are concerned,competition consists legitimately in attempting to secure favourable name brand recognition Such recognition,while an advantage,is not an unfair one Indeed,adoption of a prohibition on the joint use of the wire services provider's name and logo could well place the incumbent utilities in an unfairly disadvantageous competitive position relative to retailers affiliated with out-of-province utilities or with other potential new independent retailers,all of which are free to reap the benefits of any name brand recognition they may already enjoy It should be noted that the REAs assuming jurisdiction to create a separate corporate entity in the first instance may not be able to take advantage of this provision 55

We have not chosen to include,in section 6.2,a script of the text that must be used as the disclaimer accompanying the use of a corporate name or logo Other jurisdictions have taken this more prescriptive approach and have,for that purpose,used text along the following lines:

 

"Name of Competitive Retail Affiliate is not the same company as Name of Wire Services Provider and is not regulated by the Alberta Energy Utilities Board You do not have to buy Name of Competitive Retail Affiliate goods or services in order to continue to receive quality regulated goods or services from Name of Wire Services Provider"

 

The inclusion of such a script could help to minimize the use of crafty language by wire services providers and could be added to the ARC if the Department considers it appropriate While we have included a requirement to the effect that the disclaimer be in legible type and appear at the first instance of use,addressing examples of behaviour known to have occurred in other jurisdictions,56 there does not appear to be any ready means of completely neutralizing the creativity that corporate entities can bring to bear in respect of advertising matters

With respect to section 6.5,we have proposed that the entity responsible for licensing retailers also be responsible for preparing and maintaining lists of retailers This should not be a burdensome obligation for that entity and it avoids including in the ARC detailed provisions respecting the manner in which the lists must be prepared,if left to be prepared by the wire services provider i.e.,in alphabetical/random order,without different type face for the reference to the retail affiliate,etc

 


Section 7-Information:The provisions of this section are designed to:

ensure that a wire services provider does not act in such a manner as to provide its retail affiliate with information,obtained by virtue of its monopoly position,in preference to the information provided to non-affiliated retailers;and

protect the privacy of confidential customer information

 

With respect to section 7.1,we have taken the approach that a wire services provider cannot release"proprietary customer information",as defined in section 2 of the ARC,without the prior written consent of the customer This is consistent with the position expressed by the Department in subsections 12 1 and 12 2 of the draft Regulation for Roles,Relationship and Responsibilities of Wire Owners,Wire Service Providers and Retailers in fact,the provisions are duplicative of one another

Certain jurisdictions have taken an"all-or-none"approach to the issue,stipulating that where customer information is requested to be released to one retailer,it must be released to all Although certain Alberta stakeholders have expressed views that may tend towards such an approach,we have not followed suit One reason lies in the fact that municipalities and certain of their emanations will become subject to Albertas Freedom of Information and Protection of Privacy Act on October 1,1999 More specifically,the provisions of this Act will be applicable not only to the municipalities themselves but also to"any board,committee,commission,panel,agency or corporation that is created or owned by a municipality and all the members or officers of which are appointed or chosen by,or under the authority of,the municipality"57 Any municipally-owned wire services provider that falls within the scope of this description would therefore be required to comply with the privacy provisions of the Act The general thrust of the relevant provisions of that Act is to prohibit subject to enumerated exceptions the disclosure to third parties of non-public"personal information"unless the person to whom the information relates consents to the disclosure 58 It therefore appears appropriate for the ARC to be consistent with the legislative provisions of the Act We note,however,that to the extent there is a desire to include different rules in the ARC i.e.,if disclosed to one retailer,the information must be disclosed to all,a regulation made pursuant to paragraph 88 1 r of the Act could give precedence to the provisions of the ARC once they themselves have been embodied in a regulation

We have,however,followed the"all-or-none"approach for most information that is not"proprietary customer information"see section 7.3,while retaining an exception that permits more limited exchanges where necessary for the provision of shared corporate support services see section 7.4

10 Section 8Compliance Plans:The codes of conduct used in other jurisdictions more often than not provide for the utility to file with the regulator a compliance plan in which the utility is required to indicate the manner in which it has implemented the various provisions of the relevant code and to demonstrate that it is in compliance with the code The Enron Draft takes up this obligation and,to date,stakeholders have not expressed strong positions on the matter one way or another

Preparation of a compliance plan serves a number of useful purposes First,it requires the party preparing the plan to clearly focus its attention on affiliate relationship issues and on implementation of appropriate mechanisms against which compliance with the code of conduct can be gauged Second,it avoids to some degree having to address,within the code of conduct,methods of implementation at a high level of detail Third,it provides the party bound by the code a reasonable measure of flexibility in terms of implementation Fourth,if the compliance plan is open to inspection by third parties,the mechanism increases the pool of potential"policemen",thereby facilitating enforcement These advantages do,however,impose additional administrative costs and burdens on the utility

In drafting the compliance plan provisions of the ARC,we have attempted to achieve a sensible balance between the costs and benefits associated with compliance plans For example,we do not require,as is the case with other codes,that compliance plans be prepared and filed annually with the regulator It should also be noted that the ARC does not otherwise impose,as is the case in other jurisdictions,a requirement for all utility-affiliate transactions to be subject to a formal services agreement with all of the related administrative and transaction costs that would be engendered as a result

We see some merit in including,within the text of the ARC,a narrative description of the information required to be contained in the compliance plan,particularly if the plans are not required to be filed with the EUB or some other body This serves to provide some guidance to stakeholders as to what is expected and may also serve to provide some degree of homogeneity in form and content,if not in substantive detail,as amongst stakeholders

One issue with respect to which we have grappled is whether or not to require the utility to file its compliance plan with the EUB,the Market Surveillance Administrator or some other entity for approval The advantage of doing so is that it provides the utility with a greater measure of certainty as to the adequacy of its internal compliance measures This may be particularly important if the utility is to be subject to potentially heavy fines for non-compliance with the ARC The primary and most obvious disadvantage is that this would create a considerable additional regulatory burden It seems clear from the comments expressed by stakeholders that the EUB does not presently have the resources to review and approve numerous and potentially lengthy compliance plans on a timely basis We therefore take a middle ground approach by requiring that compliance plans be open to inspection rather than filed with a regulating entity,coupled with a right to file a complaint with a regulating entity as to the adequacy or sufficiency of a given compliance plan

11 Section 9Compliance Officer:Although not typical of North American codes of conduct,we recommend for inclusion in the ARC a provision that imposes compliance monitoring obligations on a"competent person"appointed by the wire services provider In England and Wales,this mechanism is used as a means of promoting compliance with licence obligations

Our experience in related matters reveals that this is a good means of ensuring that a utility focuses due attention on monitoring internal compliance with regulatory obligations Moreover,to the extent that the compliance officer is required to report on a regular basis to the Board of Directors,members of the Board will be aware of compliance issues and can take quick remedial action in appropriate cases

12 Section 10Audits and Record-Keeping:The codes of conduct used in other jurisdictions almost invariably require annual or bi-annual audits designed to verify compliance with the provisions of the relevant code This is an important element in enforcing and maintaining the credibility of the code of conduct as a useful mechanism for controlling behaviour,and we recommend that it be included in the ARC

IPCAA supported in this regard by the SPPA has proposed that the audit be conducted"under the control of customers and not the utilities of sic their so-called auditors"From a practical perspective,we question the ability of customers to get together to appoint and/or instruct auditors Moreover,we consider that the requirement that the audit be conducted by independent auditors in accordance with generally accepted auditing principles is adequate in the circumstances During our meetings with stakeholders,IPCAA also suggested that the audit should be at the expense of customers and not shareholders The cost of auditing a companys records is traditionally borne by the shareholders and we do not consider that it is appropriate or necessary to shift this cost to customers in order to satisfy the objectives of the ARC

The ARC includes several provisions that impose record-keeping and reporting obligations on wire services providers The detailed record-keeping and reporting obligations are reasonably necessary for the due enforcement of the ARC They promote transparency and enable,to a large extent,the industry to police itself on an ongoing basis with respect to compliance with the ARC They therefore assist in minimizing regulatory costs That said,the reporting and record-keeping requirements impose additional administrative and other costs on wire services providers These costs may be quite substantial PG&E,for example,under rules that are similar to those contained in the ARC,recently filed a compliance audit report that runs to over 200 pages and an affiliate transactions report that runs to over 800 pages

13 Section 12Enforcement and Penalties:It is convenient to consider the issue of enforcement as being comprised of three sub-issues,namely:

 

1 should enforcement be criminal or administrative

 

2 what entity should be tasked with enforcement

 

3 what should the enforcement measures be

These are,of course,interrelated questions and a particular response to one may dictate the outcome of one or both of the others For example,a decision to provide for enforcement pursuant to the criminal law in other words,to make contravention of the ARC an offence would require that the entity tasked with enforcement be the Provincial Court of Alberta or a justice or judge thereof and that the enforcement measures be either a fine or imprisonment or both,the whole of which in accordance with the provisions of the Alberta Provincial Offences Procedure Act That said,it is nonetheless useful to initially discuss the three sub-issues separately

Criminal Versus Administrative Enforcement:A number of legislative enactments stipulate that contravention of the legislation is an offence,punishable upon conviction by fine and/or imprisonment Section 74 of the EUA,for example,makes it an offence,punishable by fine,to knowingly contravene a provision of the EUA or to fail to comply with an order of the EUB 59


Other than the case of self-regulating professions and securities exchanges,administrative enforcement in the sense of the imposition of fines by administrative agencies is a relatively recent phenomenon in Canadian law and is one that appears to be gaining favour over time A good example is section 223 of the Alberta Environmental Protection and Enhancement Act,the implementation details of which are found in the Administrative Penalty Regulation Of greater direct relevance to this report are the provisions of the EUA and the Market Surveillance Regulation empowering the Power Pool Council to levy fines

We do not believe that proceeding by means of the criminal law is either necessary or appropriate with respect to enforcement of the ARC,except perhaps as a backstop in cases where a market participant fails to abide by an order of the enforcement agency made pursuant to the ARC for example,if the enforcement agency orders a market participant to cease a marketing practice and the market participant nonetheless persists in the offending behaviour Such an approach has,in our view,three principal drawbacks

The first is that enforcement would be at the discretion of the criminal justice system,which may have little time or energy to devote to administrative or regulatory offences Second,for the reasons set forth below,we have concerns as to the appropriateness of the courts as the entity responsible for the ARC Third,such an approach would be inconsistent with that already taken by the Government of Alberta with respect to conduct that threatens the competitiveness of the electricity markets More specifically,and as described in greater detail below,the enforcement scheme for conduct that is subject to surveillance by the Power Pool Council the"Council"through the Market Surveillance Administrator the"MSA"involves the criminal justice system only to the extent necessary to give full effect to an order of the Council

What Entity Should Be Tasked with Enforcement:The industry is divided on the issue of the selection of the appropriate body for enforcement purposes

 

There are essentially six options available for consideration:

 

the Department;

 

the Alberta courts of justice or a judge thereof;

 

a new entity created for the purpose of enforcing the ARC;

 

the EUB;

 

the MSA;and

 

some combination of the above

 

The Department

No stakeholder has to date suggested that the Department would be the appropriate entity for enforcement purposes,nor is it our understanding that this is a role that the Department wishes to play Enforcement of the ARC will inevitably require the conduct of hearings in certain cases,which the Department is not likely to have the resources to conduct Moreover,it would not in our view be appropriate or desirable for a government department to assume an adjudicative function over the industry Accordingly,we would not propose to pursue this option

The Civil Judicial System

We reiterate that this is an option recommended by some portion of the new entrant/large consumer group IPCAA has proposed that enforcement be given over to a judge of the Alberta Court of Queen's Bench The SPPA,while supporting the use of Alberta's justice system,also suggests as an alternative an independent authority with industry experience 60

For the reasons expressed below,we remain of the view that the civil courts do not represent the most appropriate enforcement mechanism

 

Judicial systems are notoriously slow due to back-logs in case loads A regulatory body,on the other hand,can be mandated and structured to be less formal,speedier and less expensive although in certain fields the complexity of the issues and the sums at stake can preclude this kind of advantage


The courts can make no claim to particular expertise in respect of the electricity sector generally or of the more specific competition-related issues addressed by the ARC By contrast,specially qualified personnel can be appointed to a regulatory body,and those that do not start off specially qualified can acquire experience and expertise in the regulated field The development of a specialized body that is given the power to initiate proceedings,to undertake investigations,to do research,and to play an educative and policy-formulating role as well as an adjudicative one is often an important element in the creation of an efficient regulatory regime


The core jurisdiction of the civil courts is to adjudicate disputes between parties wherein one is aggrieved by the conduct of another As such,the remedial powers of the civil courts focus on the award of damages to the aggrieved party by way of compensation for the harm suffered by that party 61 Accordingly,it is not in the tradition of the civil judicial system to impose fines or penalties for non-compliance with regulatory obligations,62 nor does it appear to us to be appropriate to augment the remedial powers of the courts to include such a remedy in non-criminal cases


One could imagine a situation,for example,where a market participant is in breach of the ARC such as the failure to file a compliance plan but there is no damage suffered by a third party or parties In such a case,a remedy that might be available to the courts on the basis of their traditional jurisdiction is the issuance of an order compelling the market participant to file a compliance plan There having been no damage sustained by any third party,however,no financial consequence would follow

Accordingly,we do not recommend that the Alberta justice system be the entity responsible for enforcement of the ARC That said,we have expressly reserved in the ARC the right of parties to seek civil relief in appropriate cases In other words,to the extent that the breach by one market participant of the provisions of the ARC has,in fact,caused damage to another market participant,we do not preclude the use of the justice system by the aggrieved market participant as a means of obtaining compensation We also propose to retain the courts as a backstop for enforcing orders made under the ARC

IPCAA has proposed that enforcement be given over to a justice of the Court of Queen's Bench We understand from discussions with the IPCAA representative while in Calgary that the proposal that a judge be designated as the enforcement body is being made with a particular person or persons in mind Our primary difficulty with this approach remains a practical one Specifically,there is no guarantee that a particular person or persons would be either willing to serve in this capacity or would be available on a on-going basis to hear complaints as and when they arise Absent a designated person in other words,simply indicating that"a judge"will be empowered to enforce the ARC,there is no reasonable assurance that the person designated in any given case would have the necessary industry expertise Moreover,we believe that there is merit in consistency in decision-making on these issues and would therefore prefer that an established body assume the enforcement role

A New Independent Enforcement Authority

The proposal put forward by the SPPA that a knowledgeable independent authority 63 be established does not present the difficulties noted above Rather,the central issue here is whether or not enforcement of the ARC warrants the creation of a new entity Both TransAlta and ATCO are opposed to the establishment of an additional regulator for purposes of the enforcement of the ARC 64

Alberta already has three regulatory bodies involved in the electricity industry;namely,the Department,the EUB and the MSA It is not clear that a fourth is required or justified,unless the existing entities are unsuitable,either by reason of constrained resources,jurisdiction or otherwise As noted below,however,one of the existing entities-the MSA-appears to us not only to be appropriate in general terms but to have been established with a view to undertaking this particular function among others In addition,as also noted below,the MSA has the statutory jurisdiction to address issues arising from the ARC Indeed,the MSA must investigate any complaint respecting"any aspect of the relationship between the owner of an electric distribution system and its affiliated retailers"or retailers other than its affiliated retailers 65 While the MSA may stop an investigation if another body"has the ability to provide a more effective remedy than the MSA or the Power Pool Council can provide"under the EUA or the Market Surveillance Regulation,66 there remains some opportunity for"forum shopping"by market participants that,for one reason or another,are dissatisfied with the independent authority

Accordingly,while we see some merit in the SPPA's proposal,absent some compelling reason for dismissing the MSA as the enforcement agency,we are not convinced that the establishment of a new entity is warranted at this time

The EUB and the MSA/Council

Division of Enforcement Responsibilities:We propose to divide responsibility for enforcement of the ARC between the MSA and the EUB That said,we suggest that the MSA should have the principal role in enforcing the ARC and propose to limit the role of the EUB to enforcement of those sections of the ARC sections 4.2,4.4,4.5,5.1 and 5.2 that deal more specifically and directly with cross-subsidization This appears to us to mirror what we understand to be the conceptual framework of the legislative scheme wherein the EUB has as a principal role the regulatory oversight of regulated entities with a view to protecting consumers in terms of matters relating to rates including cross-subsidization while the MSA/Council has as a principal role the regulatory oversight of unregulated entities with a view to protecting or promoting competition 67

Moreover,empowering the EUB to address the above-noted sections of the ARC will limit the potential for inconsistent decisions on similar issues between the MSA,when enforcing the ARC,and the EUB,when exercising its powers with respect to rates For example,the EUB may well be called upon in its rate proceedings to address the question of the transfer price in respect of the transfer of assets and services between the regulated and unregulated businesses of a utility There is therefore merit in this element of the ARC being subject to EUB oversight

In selecting the MSA as the principal enforcement agency,we have been mindful of the concerns expressed by certain stakeholders as to the adequacy of the EUB's resources for purposes of coping with enforcement of the ARC 68 Although certain stakeholders have also dismissed the MSA as an enforcement agency for purposes of the ARC,few have expressed the reasons for such dismissal 69 EPCOR has,however,noted the potential difficulties surrounding the fact that the MSA has no remedial powers and is limited to making recommendations to the Power Pool Council We return to this latter point below

We have also been compelled by the fact that the statutory jurisdiction of the MSA includes surveillance of exactly the matters at issue in the ARC More specifically,the relevant portions of section 9.1 of the EUA read as follows:

 

"9.1 1 The Power Pool Council shall,in accordance with the regulations,carry out surveillance in respect of any activity related to the engaging in,or proposing to engage in,the generation,transmission,distribution,exchange,purchase or sale of electricity,including the surveillance of



b any aspect of the relationship between the owner of an electric distribution system and its affiliated retailers,


c any aspect of the relationship between the owner of an electric distribution system and retailers other than its affiliate retailers,



to determine whether or not


f the results of that activity are efficient and equitable,


g the persons carrying out that activity are complying with this Act,the regulationsand any arrangements entered into under this Act or the regulations,and


h the rules,guidelines and conventions that govern the Alberta electric industry are sufficient to discourage eligible persons from employing anti-competitive practices in that industry



3 The Market Surveillance Administrator shall carry out the surveillance duties referred to in subsection 1,but the Power Pool Council is not precluded from carrying out those duties or exercising any other duty or function described in sections 9.2,9.3 and 9.4"

Finally,we are not troubled by the fact that the Market Surveillance Regulation contains a"sunset clause"since the EUA does not itself contemplate,nor would we expect,the disappearance of the MSA or the Council To the extent that the ARC repeats any relevant provisions of the Market Surveillance Regulation,even the repeal of that Regulation will not affect the scheme being put into place to enforce the ARC

General Scheme of MSA:The EUA and the Market Surveillance Regulation contemplate that the MSA will be the investigative arm of the Council although,as noted in the above citation,the Council retains authority to conduct any investigation directly As such,the MSA has no specific power to order any form of relief Rather,its sole power is to make recommendations to the Council Upon receipt of a report,the Council may accept,modify or reject a recommendation of the MSA;hold a hearing into the matter;and impose fines or other sanctions set out in the regulations 70

EPCOR has raised the question of the utility of entrusting enforcement of the ARC to an entity that has only investigative powers and has no authority to order the imposition of sanctions 71 As a practical matter,the MSA is in essence performing a function akin to that of the investigative and expert economic or other advisory staff of many regulatory bodies 72 Accordingly,one would not expect the division of responsibilities as between the MSA and the Council as a whole to,in and of itself,unduly protract the consideration of complaints Moreover,the separation of the investigative function from the adjudicative function does provide some measure of immunity from arguments that the Council as a whole is acting as both prosecutor and judge in any given case

Mandatory Duty:As noted above,the MSA's statutory duty is a mandatory one In other words,it must investigate any matter that is within its admittedly very broad jurisdiction that is brought to its attention by way of referral or complaint from any source and may,in addition,initiate investigations on its own initiative As also noted above,however,the MSA must terminate an investigation if the complaint or referral relates to a matter that is within the jurisdiction of another body and,in the opinion of the MSA,the other body"has the ability to provide a more effective remedy than the MSA or the Council can provide"under the EUA or the regulations

Overlapping Jurisdiction:This raises the issue of a potential overlap or duplication of jurisdiction between the MSA,on the one hand,and the EUB and other bodies,notably federal competition authorities,on the other Such overlap can lead to duplication of process and to inconsistent decisions by different regulatory bodies on essentially the same issue We understand that there have been discussions amongst these entities and that there may be a Memorandum of Understanding between them as to which should take the lead role in respect of any given matter where there may be some element of overlap We have not been provided with a copy of such Memorandum of Understanding assuming that one exists but note that it should be reviewed prior to finalization of the ARC in order to ensure that the ARC is not inconsistent with the terms of that document

Powers and Remedies of MSA/Council:The fact that considerable thought has already been given to vesting the MSA and the Council,respectively,with quite substantial enforcement powers is not an insignificant advantage at this point in time From a legal perspective,empowering regulatory bodies is something of a tricky business Among others,due regard needs to be paid to issues relating to natural justice in other words,due process,the Charter of Rights and Freedoms and the provisions of section 96 of the Constitution Act,1867 relating to the jurisdiction and powers that may constitutionally be vested in inferior courts and administrative agencies Legislative counsel in Alberta undoubtedly considered these issues in detail when drafting the Market Surveillance Regulation in particular,and we would therefore not propose to repeat that exercise for purposes of this report

The investigative powers of the MSA and the remedial powers of the Council are quite extensive and encompass the following:

 

The MSA has broad powers to make inquiries and obtain documentation in furtherance of its investigations,with a right to seek a court order for the production of documents and information from non-compliant market participants 73


The MSA may make use of the services of the staff of the Council or of persons having special technical or other knowledge or skills 74


The MSA must include in its report an indiaction of whether its investigation has disclosed any activity that substantially affects the establishment,maintenance or operation of a competitive and efficient market for electricity and electricity services and any information relating to the matters specified in paragraphs 9.1 1 f,g and h cited above 75 This appears to us to provide a useful additional measure of oversight in terms of the on-going relevance and adequacy of the ARC in achieving its stated objectives of promoting fair competition and non-discriminatory access

The Council may do any of the following:i accept,modify or reject a recommendation of the MSA;ii hold a hearing into the matter;iii impose fines or other sanctions set out in the regulations;or iv refer the matter to any of the entities noted in subsection 9.5 2 of the EUA 76 Where the Council decides to hold a hearing or decides or is required to refer the matter to another entity,the Council may make interim decisions in certain cases 77

The Council has the power to compel the attendance of witnesses and the production of documents by witnesses,and is not bound by the rules of evidence applicable in court proceedings

We propose to incorporate by reference into the ARC most of the procedural provisions set forth in the Market Surveillance Regulation

What Should the Enforcement Measures Be:One issue with respect to which there appears to be some degree of consensus amongst stakeholders is with respect to the need for strong enforcement measures to"give teeth"to the ARC

Other Jurisdictions:In terms of international practice,we note that the codes of conduct of other jurisdictions,to the extent that they speak to the issue of enforcement,often provide for two principal sanctions;namely,the issuance of"cease and desist"orders and the imposition of financial penalties This is the case with California,Texas,and Illinois 78 New Jersey,Maine and Massachusetts provide for monetary penalties 79 The codes of conduct of each of California,New Jersey,Illinois,and Maine incorporate the following additional sanctions:

In California,each violation of the rules relating to non-discrimination,disclosure of/access to information and separation counts as a point against the utility Where a utility accumulates three or more points,the California Public Utilities Commission may impose a one-year prohibition on the utility entering into any transactions including sales of tariffed or non-tariffed services with any affiliate s involved in the violation


In New Jersey,a violation of the cost allocation rules may result in the utility being required to reimburse its ratepayers an amount equal to the unfairly allocated costs,with interest Where a utility has committed a"substantial violation"that has conferred an unfair competitive advantage,the New Jersey Board of Public Utilities must order the utility to a offer services through a functionally or structurally separate business unit or b divest itself of any business unit providing such services Recurring and significant violations are subject to"increasingly stringent action"


In Illinois,the Illinois Commerce Commission may alter,modify or suspend the retail affiliate's certificate of service authority for substantial or repeated violations of or non-conformance with the affiliate transaction rules


In Maine,divestiture of a retail affiliate may be ordered where a the utility or the affiliate has knowingly violated the code of conduct and the violation resulted or had the potential to result in substantial injury to retail consumers or to the competitive retail market or b the retail affiliate obtained an unfair market advantage as a result of an entity's ownership of the stock of the utility

The codes of conduct of the various other jurisdictions have not been in place long enough to permit a meaningful analysis of the effectiveness of any one or more of the enforcement measures contained therein However,the above list illustrates the mechanisms that the regulators in other jurisdictions have determined appropriate to both sanction and deter non-compliant behaviour

Financial Penalties:There seems little doubt that financial penalties should be among the enforcement measures available to sanction non-compliance with the ARC The question,then,is the quantum of the penalty

Pursuant to subsection 74 1 of the EUA,the maximum penalty for a breach of the EUA is 500,000.00 payable on conviction A person found guilty of non-compliance with an order of the Board is subject to a fine of up to 100,000.00 a day for every day during which the default continues The Market Surveillance Regulation,for its part,provides that violations of the EUA,the Market Surveillance Regulation,the rules of the power pool or any arrangement entered into under the EUA or the Market Surveillance Regulation is punishable by fine among other sanctions The Regulation establishes neither a minimum nor a maximum amount,the quantum being left to be determined by the Council on a case-by-case basis depending upon what,in any given case,is"sufficient to deter the market participant from engaging in similar conduct in the future"Non-compliance with an order of the Council is an offence punishable by a fine of not more than 100,000.00 per day for every day during which the non-compliance continues

The Enron Draft proposes penalties of not less than $5,000.00 and not more than 20,000.00 for each violation,with each day of a"continuing violation"being considered a separate violation if the Board so determines It is,in our view,preferable to refrain from establishing a minimum fine While penalties should be sufficient to discourage inappropriate behaviour,they should have a rational basis and not be excessive in any given case A minimum fine of $5,000.00,as suggested in the Enron Draft,could be excessive in the case of small violations of the ARC that do not or are not likely to have an appreciable effect on competitors,customers or competition in the marketplace

We also believe that there is merit in not establishing a maximum fine,leaving it to the discretion of the Council or the Board,as the case may be,to fix the level of fine having regard to certain criteria The absence of a maximum amount may have a greater deterrent effect in the sense that stakeholders cannot gauge in advance the cost of non-compliance in any given case In other words,a stakeholder that might be prepared to risk a 500,000.00 fine for non-compliant behaviour that it feels may,in the short or medium term,result in some net gain or advantage that is worth more than 500,000.00 Whether or not such will be the case,however,will depend in large part on the stakeholder community's perception of the willingness of the enforcement agency to impose significant fines in appropriate cases We are not in a position to assess to what extent this is the case in Alberta Thus,while we have not included a maximum fine for the moment,this is an issue that could be revisited

Other Sanctions:Certain additional sanctions used in other jurisdictions notably divestiture are not suited to the Alberta legislative environment and need not be considered further

The Enron Draft incorporates a temporary ban on affiliate transactions along the lines of that found in the California affiliate transaction rules The Enron Draft also incorporates as a sanction a prospective limitation on the amount,percentage or value of transactions entered into between a wire services provider and its retail affiliate 80 While we see some merit to sanctions of this nature,we have some concerns as to how they may affect customers Until such time as the default supply and supplier of last resort questions have been finally determined,we cannot assess the full implication of the Enron proposal Accordingly,we prefer at this time to take a less prescriptive approach by empowering the Council to impose any terms and conditions that it considers appropriate on the market participant's activities Such a remedial power is sufficiently broad to permit an affiliate transaction ban or a limit on affiliate transactions in appropriate circumstances

We also see merit in the provision of the Enron Draft that requires a wire services provider to publicly announce to customers that it has been found to be in violation of the ARC This has the potential to serve as a useful deterrent to non-compliance We do,however,see some difficulty in the billing packet mechanism proposed to be used since the wire services provider will not always be responsible for billing We therefore suggest as a more flexible alternative a provision that empowers the Council to order the wire services provider or its affiliate to provide notice of the violation in such manner as the Council determines appropriate

We have incorporated from the Market Surveillance Regulation a provision that empowers the Council to order a market participant to cease from acting in non-compliance with the ARC Under the Market Surveillance Regulation,this remedy is only available following a hearing by the Council There may,however,be instances where issuance of a temporary restraining order i.e.,a cease and desist order issued prior to the outcome of a hearing may be desirable An example might be where a competitive retail affiliate is continuously making use of the wire services provider's name or logo without a disclaimer or with an inappropriate or inadequate disclaimer The difficulty here is that,while the Council is empowered by the EUA to make interim decisions,the grounds upon which it can do so,described in paragraphs 9.7 2 a and 9.7 2 b of the EUA,are not applicable to affiliate relationship matters 81 Accordingly,unless the EUA is amended or a regulation is enacted by the Lieutenant Governor in Council pursuant to paragraph 72 1 d of the EUA respecting any matter that the Minister considers is not provided for or is insufficiently provided for in the EUA,it would not appear to be possible to empower the Council to issue interim decisions for purposes of enforcing the ARC

Finally,we also incorporate the provision of the Market Surveillance Regulation that imposes liability on corporate officers that participate or acquiesce in a violation of the ARC

14 Section 13Exceptions and Exemptions:With respect to exemptions,we suggest that empowering an agency to allow exemptions on a case-by-case basis is preferable to attempting to list exemptions within the body of the ARC itself With few exceptions,it is not likely possible to ascertain at this time the need for specific exemptions from the application of the ARC,whether in whole or more likely in part 82 An exemption mechanism,while creating an additional regulatory burden for the relevant agency,provides an important measure of flexibility to deal with changes in the industry as and when they occur,without the need to amend the regulation embodying the ARC We have,however,included in section 13.1 an indication of the factors to be considered in granting exemptions

We note that ATCO has expressed its opposition to exemptions designed specifically to provide some measure of relief for smaller,inexperienced utilitie s 83

15 Section 14Review:We assume that the regulation embodying the ARC will contain the clause often found in Alberta regulations to the effect that the regulation will expire on a certain date unless repassed in its present or an amended form following a review We support the inclusion of such a provision,which will afford a useful opportunity to review the effectiveness of the ARC and to either strengthen or relax its provisions as may be appropriate having regard to the outcome of the review

 

